	abstract = {Surfaces containing 4 ÃÂ 8 ÃÂ 40 ÎØEm staggered rhombus posts were hydrophobized using two methods. One, using a dimethyldichlorosilane reaction in the vapor phase, introduces a smooth modified layer, and the other, a solution reaction using methyltrichlorosilane, imparts a second (nanoscopic) length scale of topography. The smooth modified surface exhibits contact angles of ÎžA/ÎžR = 176{\textbackslash},$^{\textrm{{\textbackslash}circ}}$/156{\textbackslash},$^{\textrm{{\textbackslash}circ}}$. Arguments are made that the pinning of the receding contact line by the post tops (with ÎžA/ÎžR = 104{\textbackslash},$^{\textrm{{\textbackslash}circ}}$/103{\textbackslash},$^{\textrm{{\textbackslash}circ}}$) is responsible for the hysteresis. The second level of topography raises the contact angles of the post tops and the macroscopic sample to ÎžA/ÎžR = {\textbackslash}textgreater176{\textbackslash},$^{\textrm{{\textbackslash}circ}}$/{\textbackslash}textgreater176{\textbackslash},$^{\textrm{{\textbackslash}circ}}$ and eliminates hysteresis. The increase in Laplace pressure due to the increase in the advancing contact angle of the post tops is a second reason that two length scales of topography are important.},
	number = {7},
