	abstract = {A micromachined specimen with a test section only 150- mu m thick was developed for investigating subcritical crack growth in silicon. Crack growth rates in the range 10sup -4-10sup -10 m/s were measured as a function of applied stress intensity ( nu -K curves) during tests in humid air and dry nitrogen lasting up to 24 h. The fracture toughness, Ksub Ic of 110 silicon was also measured at 1.15+or-0.08 MPa msup 1/2. While some evidence MPa-msup 1/2 of subcritical crack growth appeared to occur in the region 0.9 Ksub Ic{\textless}K{\textless}0.98 Ksub Ic, the extremely high crack growth exponent (n{\textgreater}100) and the high ratio of the apparent stress corrosion threshold, Ksub Iscc, to the fracture toughness, Ksub Iscc/Ksub Ic{\textgreater}0.9, suggests that no clear evidence exists for a stress corrosion process in silicon exposed to humid air. (43 refs.)},
	language = {English},
	number = {3},
	journal = {Journal of Materials Research},
	publisher = {USA : Mater. Res. Soc, 2002},
